# Frontend ownership (FE1 and FE2)

The full agreement is docs/frontend-coordination.md. Read it before changing routes, shared files, styles, or API code in frontend/. API field names follow the contracts on main: docs/DATABASE_SCHEMA.md and backend/app/schemas/.

Frontend Developer 2 owns these paths:
- frontend/src/features/auth/, frontend/src/features/reviewForm/, frontend/src/features/recommendations/
- frontend/src/components/form/
- frontend/src/api/client.ts, frontend/src/api/errors.ts, frontend/src/api/appClient.ts, frontend/src/api/recommendations.ts, frontend/src/api/mock/
- frontend/src/config/env.ts, frontend/src/domain/reviewVocabulary.ts, frontend/src/routes/paths.ts, frontend/src/routes/fe2Routes.tsx

Frontend Developer 1 owns everything else in frontend/, including App.tsx, main.tsx, index.css, vite.config.ts, index.html, src/pages/, and src/api/courses.ts. frontend/src/api/reviews.ts is shared: FE1 writes the review list call, FE2 writes the create and worth-score calls. frontend/src/styles/exampleB.css is shared: it holds the Example B design tokens; either developer can add tokens. frontend/e2e/ and frontend/playwright.config.ts are shared: the Playwright suite (#32); either developer adds specs for their pages using e2e/fixtures.ts.

When working in frontend/:
- Do not edit the other developer's paths without that developer's approval.
- Keep edits to shared files (App.tsx, index.css, package.json, vite.config.ts) as small as possible, and list them in the PR description.
- Never hand-merge package-lock.json. Take the base branch's copy and run npm install.
- Build URLs with the helpers in src/routes/paths.ts, not string literals. Course URLs use the course's UUID.
- Take review goal, Worth Score, difficulty, weekly hours, semester, and match-level keys and labels from src/domain/reviewVocabulary.ts.
- Read auth state through useAuth() from src/features/auth, and get API clients from useApiClient().
- Show "No reviews yet" when community_worth_score is null, never 0.
- Before a frontend PR, run npm test and npm run e2e in frontend/. End-to-end tests answer API calls from e2e/fakeBackend.ts; use backend.respondOnce() for error cases rather than a real server.
- The team's design is Example B (white + warm, Figtree). Style new UI with the --wtc-* variables from src/styles/exampleB.css, each with a fallback, e.g. var(--wtc-ink, #1e2124). Orange fills take ink text, not white; orange text uses --wtc-link.
